gma_race": 7.0, "sigma_total": 7.72, "baseline_margin": -10.44, "analyst_adjustment": -0.56, "key_factors": ["R+10 district with incumbent John Rutherford running for re-election", "All nine expert ratings place the seat in the Republican column, mostly Safe or Solid R", "Rutherford holds a large fundraising edge with no outside spending against him", "No public general-election polling; Polymarket prices Grage at about 7 percent"], "rationale": "The district's ten-point Republican lean, Rutherford's incumbency, and his clear fundraising advantage outweigh any national tailwind for Democrats, and every expert rating plus prediction-market pricing puts the seat firmly in Republican hands.", "watch": "A decisive national wave toward Democrats could narrow the margin, and with no local polling the true size of any swing is the main unknown.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent John Rutherford (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Florida's 5th District leans Republican by about ten points, and incumbent John Rutherford is seeking another term after winning his party's nomination in August. Rachel Grage took the Democratic nomination, but she enters the general election with far less money, roughly $272,000 raised to Rutherford's $727,000, and no visible outside spending on either side. The U.S. Chamber of Commerce backed Rutherford ahead of a primary that drew some local controversy over campaign flyers.\n\nNo public polls of the general election are available, so the outlook rests on the district's fundamentals. Every major race rater places the seat in the Republican column, most calling it Safe or Solid, and prediction markets give Grage only about a 7 percent chance. The main uncertainty is how large any national swing toward Democrats proves to be, but the district's lean and incumbency make Rutherford a clear favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0443,